.TH TUXDISPLAY 1 "September 2026" "TuxDisplay 0.4.6" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tuxdisplay \- use an iPad as a secondary Linux display
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tuxdisplay
.RI [ command ]
.PP
.B tuxdisplay
.B launch
.I application
.RI [ argument ...]
.PP
.B tuxdisplay
.B usb
.BR connect | disconnect | status
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B TuxDisplay
creates a compositor-owned virtual monitor in a GNOME Wayland session and
streams it to the OpenDisplay iPad app through usbmuxd. This direct USB mode
requires no IP address, Internet connection, Wi-Fi, cellular service, or
Personal Hotspot. Windows can be moved between the physical display and iPad.
.PP
An authenticated browser viewer is available on trusted private networks.
Xorg and unsupported Wayland compositors use an isolated X11/noVNC workspace;
that workspace is not an extension of the current desktop.
.PP
Version 0.4.0 must not be used on GNOME Wayland because its touch path could
abort Mutter. Versions 0.4.3 and 0.4.4 have connection regressions. Use 0.4.6 or newer.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B gui
Open the standalone GTK manager. This is the default when no command is given.
.TP
.B start
Start the per-user service and create the selected display backend.
.TP
.B stop
Stop streaming and remove the virtual monitor or fallback workspace.
.TP
.B restart
Stop and recreate the display. OpenDisplay and browser viewers must reconnect.
.TP
.B status
Show service state, display mode, OpenDisplay connection state, browser
fallback URL, and access PIN.
.TP
.B url
Print the preferred browser-fallback URL. Direct OpenDisplay USB does not use
this URL.
.TP
.B password
Show the six-digit browser access PIN.
.TP
.B password --reset
Generate a new browser PIN and invalidate the previous value after restart.
.TP
.BI "launch " "application [argument ...]"
Start an application in the current GNOME desktop. In X11 fallback mode, start
it in the isolated workspace.
.TP
.B usb connect
Start the display if necessary and wait for OpenDisplay on a trusted,
cable-connected iPad. When no iPad is paired, TuxDisplay may offer the optional
USB gadget fallback on supported device-capable hardware.
.TP
.B usb disconnect
Stop the display connection and tear down a TuxDisplay USB gadget if one was
created.
.TP
.B usb status
Report trusted-device, direct OpenDisplay, USB-network, and gadget capability
state.
.TP
.B tray
Run the desktop indicator. Its icon is gray when stopped, amber while waiting
for a receiver, and green when a receiver is viewing.
.TP
.B doctor
Check the selected display mode, required programs, networking, trusted iPad,
and USB gadget capability.
.SH DISPLAY MODES
.SS GNOME Wayland
Mutter creates a real virtual output named
.BR Meta-0 .
PipeWire captures only that output. GStreamer supplies H.264 to OpenDisplay
and JPEG frames to the browser fallback.
.SS OpenDisplay USB
TuxDisplay connects through usbmuxd to OpenDisplay port 9000. Video and input
use the public OpenDisplay version 3 framing protocol over the USB cable. No
network interface or browser PIN is involved.
.SS Browser fallback
GNOME Wayland serves an authenticated MJPEG viewer over HTTP. Use it only on
a trusted private LAN or cable network.
.SS X11 compatibility
Xvfb, Openbox, tint2, x11vnc, websockify, and noVNC provide a separate desktop.
Applications from the current desktop cannot be dragged into it.
.SH RESOLUTION
The supported presets are 1024x768, 1280x800, 1366x768, 1920x1080,
2048x1536, and 2160x1620. The default is 1920x1080.
.PP
Most traditional iPads are close to 4:3. Use 2048x1536 or 2160x1620 to reduce
letterboxing, or 1024x768 for lower CPU and bandwidth use.
.PP
Changing resolution restarts and replaces the virtual monitor. The iPad can
show its last frame briefly, and GNOME can move windows from the removed output
back to a physical monitor. Reopen OpenDisplay if it does not reconnect.
.SH INPUT
OpenDisplay touch and Apple Pencil events are translated into a guarded
single-pointer stream. Tap, drag, pointer movement, and scrolling are
supported. Native multi-touch, Pencil pressure, and tilt are not forwarded.
.PP
The browser path also accepts pointer, touch, scrolling, and keyboard events,
subject to Safari and iPadOS restrictions.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/config
accepts:
.PP
.nf
RESOLUTION=1920x1080
FPS=30
KEEP_AWAKE_WITH_LID_CLOSED=0
DISPLAY_NUMBER=48
WEB_PORT=6080
VNC_PORT=5900
.fi
.PP
.B DISPLAY_NUMBER
and
.B VNC_PORT
apply only to the X11 fallback.
.B FPS
accepts 15, 30, or 60 and controls the virtual-monitor, encoder, and advertised
receiver cadence. The default is 30.
.B KEEP_AWAKE_WITH_LID_CLOSED
accepts 0 or 1. When enabled, TuxDisplay blocks system sleep and lid-switch
handling while the display service runs. This also blocks manual and idle
suspend. Stopping TuxDisplay releases the inhibitor.
.B WEB_PORT
and the PIN apply only to browser access. Restart the service after editing.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TUXDISPLAY_FORCE_X11=1
Force the isolated X11/noVNC backend instead of GNOME Wayland mode.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/config
Resolution and port settings, created with private permissions.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/password
Generated browser PIN.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/*.rfb
Authentication material for the VNC fallback when used.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tuxdisplay/
Connection state and application logs.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.nf
tuxdisplay status
tuxdisplay usb status
tuxdisplay doctor
systemctl --user status tuxdisplay.service
journalctl --user -u tuxdisplay.service --since "10 minutes ago"
.fi
.SH SECURITY
OpenDisplay traffic uses the operating system's trusted-device usbmuxd channel,
but OpenDisplay protocol version 3 adds no encryption or authentication.
Trust only computers and iPads you control.
.PP
The browser service listens on local interfaces and uses PIN authentication
over ordinary HTTP. Never expose port 6080 to the public Internet or an
untrusted network.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Native desktop extension requires GNOME Wayland. Direct OpenDisplay transport
is currently USB-only. Encoding is software x264. Receiver dimensions are not
used to select or dynamically resize the virtual monitor.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tuxdisplay-usb (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
